WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
Hey everyone, Professor Dave here, I wanna talk a little more about nucleophilic substitutions

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.400
specifically, the SN1 reaction

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.800
so the SN1 is another nucleophilic substitution reaction

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.360
so it's similar to the SN2, but there's a couple key mechanistic differences.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.836
so let's take a look at what's going on

00:00:22.836 --> 00:00:26.259
the first difference here, is that it is a two-step reaction

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.520
so i know that's confusing, SN1 being two steps and SN2 being 1

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.360
but once again it's about the transition state of the reaction. so let's see here

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:37.240
so the first step is that the leaving group leaves

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:43.840
so this is prior to any contact with a nucleophile, this carbon is able to lose a bond to this chlorine

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:53.360
chloride is very stable by itself, we have a carbocation intermediate here. so one key thing to understand

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:06.540
is that we're going from an sp3 hybridized tetrahedral carbon, to an sp2 hybridized trigonal planar situation

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.940
because this carbon is losing an electron domain

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:15.480
so now that this carbon with a formal positive charge has only three electron domains is gonna have

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:21.220
trigonal planar geometry, so basically this molecule is a plane perpendicular to the board

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:28.020
we are looking at it edge on like this. now that we have the carbocation intermediate

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.380
the second step is for the nucleophile to attack

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:35.480
here let's say it's just a molecule of water solvent

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:40.540
it is gonna go ahead and attack here, and we'll have a water molecule attached

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:47.700
and then another molecule of water will go ahead, snag that proton, deprotonate, and we'll have just the alcohol

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:55.400
one very important part about the SN1 mechanism is that because a carbocation intermediate

00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:00.200
is trigonal planar, that means that a nucleophile can attack from either side

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.080
remember that any time we have a reaction occurring

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:10.380
something is dumping electrons from its highest occupied molecular orbital

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:16.480
and putting them into the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital, and in this case, if we have a trigonal planar

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:22.760
situation, the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital is the unhybridized remaining p orbital

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:30.200
so these are sp2, and then the unhybridized third p orbital is each lobe extending in this direction

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:35.580
perpendicular to the plane of the molecule. so that means that water can attack from this side

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:41.840
or water can attack from that side. and if you are generating a chiral center you are gonna get something

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.160
called a racemic mixture

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:55.380
of enantiomers, or of stereoisomers at that location

00:02:55.380 --> 00:03:02.440
which means in 50% of the resulting molecules you'll have an R stereocenter and in 50% you'll have S

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:05.219
so that's something to be mindful of.

00:03:05.219 --> 00:03:09.079
thanks for watching, guys. subscribe to my channel for more tutorials

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.080
and as always feel free to email me with questions

